/T (What the three slices return. readings[7:14] returns the 7 items at indexes 7-13, i.e. days 8 to 14, the second week \(stop index is excluded\). readings[-7:] returns the last 7 items, days 359-365 \(a negative start counts from the end\). readings[::7] returns every 7th item starting at index 0, i.e. days 1, 8, 15, ..., 365 - 53 items for a 365-day list. Each slice is a new list.)
/E (What the three slices return. readings[7:14] returns the 7 items at indexes 7-13, i.e. days 8 to 14, the second week \(stop index is excluded\). readings[-7:] returns the last 7 items, days 359-365 \(a negative start counts from the end\). readings[::7] returns every 7th item starting at index 0, i.e. days 1, 8, 15, ..., 365 - 53 items for a 365-day list. Each slice is a new list.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 112 0 R
/T (Why a list is suitable. The data are ordered by date, of one kind, and read far more often than they change, so index access \(O\(1\)\) and slicing are exactly what is needed, and one reading per day gives a natural day-number to index mapping. Measured on 365,000 readings: append 0.04 us \(amortised O\(1\)\), pop\(\) 2.21 us \(O\(1\); this figure includes timer overhead\), insert in the middle 81 us and at the front 176 us \(O\(n\): later items shift\), pop\(0\) 134 us \(O\(n\)\), sort\(\) 24.3 ms on random data \(O\(n log n\)\) but 0.9 ms on already sorted data \(Timsort is O\(n\) there\).)
/E (Why a list is suitable. The data are ordered by date, of one kind, and read far more often than they change, so index access \(O\(1\)\) and slicing are exactly what is needed, and one reading per day gives a natural day-number to index mapping. Measured on 365,000 readings: append 0.04 us \(amortised O\(1\)\), pop\(\) 2.21 us \(O\(1\); this figure includes timer overhead\), insert in the middle 81 us and at the front 176 us \(O\(n\): later items shift\), pop\(0\) 134 us \(O\(n\)\), sort\(\) 24.3 ms on random data \(O\(n log n\)\) but 0.9 ms on already sorted data \(Timsort is O\(n\) there\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
186 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 112 0 R
/T (Derivation of the number of additions. There are n - 6 windows. Naive: each window adds 7 values = 6 additions, so 6\(n - 6\) additions, which grows like 7n \(O\(7n\)\). Running sum: 6 additions for the first window, then every slide needs one addition and one subtraction, so \(n - 1\) additions and \(n - 7\) subtractions, i.e. about 2n operations, O\(n\) and independent of the window size. The counting versions in the program confirm the formulas exactly:)
/E (Derivation of the number of additions. There are n - 6 windows. Naive: each window adds 7 values = 6 additions, so 6\(n - 6\) additions, which grows like 7n \(O\(7n\)\). Running sum: 6 additions for the first window, then every slide needs one addition and one subtraction, so \(n - 1\) additions and \(n - 7\) subtractions, i.e. about 2n operations, O\(n\) and independent of the window size. The counting versions in the program confirm the formulas exactly:)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
