267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 113 0 R
/T (Comparing times with the derived counts. The addition ratio is about 6.0x \(6\(n-6\) against n-1\), but the time ratio at n = 365,000 is only 2.7x. The reason is that run time is not just additions: both versions pay Python's per-iteration overhead \(loop control, list indexing, appending to the result\), which the running sum does not remove. The count tells us how the work grows with n and w \(the naive version is O\(n*w\), the running sum O\(n\)\); it does not predict the constant factors. Both versions are linear in n: ten times more days \(36,500 to 365,000\) made the naive version 10.9x slower and the running-sum version 11.1x slower.)
/E (Comparing times with the derived counts. The addition ratio is about 6.0x \(6\(n-6\) against n-1\), but the time ratio at n = 365,000 is only 2.7x. The reason is that run time is not just additions: both versions pay Python's per-iteration overhead \(loop control, list indexing, appending to the result\), which the running sum does not remove. The count tells us how the work grows with n and w \(the naive version is O\(n*w\), the running sum O\(n\)\); it does not predict the constant factors. Both versions are linear in n: ten times more days \(36,500 to 365,000\) made the naive version 10.9x slower and the running-sum version 11.1x slower.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [35]
>>
