349 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 114 0 R
/T (reading automatically drops the oldest one in O\(1\), so there is no slicing and no index arithmetic \(code: ma_deque in the source\). With a running total the time is O\(n\); recomputing sum\(window\) for each day would be O\(7n\). Working space is O\(7\) = O\(1\) apart from the output list. Dictionary keyed by date. Readings are stored as {date: mm}; the average needs by_date[start + i*day] look-ups \(O\(1\) average each\), so the total is still O\(n\) but with a larger constant - measured 9.6x slower than the list running sum at n = 365,000 - and it uses O\(n\) space with a much larger constant \(hash table plus a date object per key\). The dictionary wins only for sparse or out-of-order data.)
/E (reading automatically drops the oldest one in O\(1\), so there is no slicing and no index arithmetic \(code: ma_deque in the source\). With a running total the time is O\(n\); recomputing sum\(window\) for each day would be O\(7n\). Working space is O\(7\) = O\(1\) apart from the output list. Dictionary keyed by date. Readings are stored as {date: mm}; the average needs by_date[start + i*day] look-ups \(O\(1\) average each\), so the total is still O\(n\) but with a larger constant - measured 9.6x slower than the list running sum at n = 365,000 - and it uses O\(n\) space with a much larger constant \(hash table plus a date object per key\). The dictionary wins only for sparse or out-of-order data.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [58]
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 114 0 R
/T (list.insert\(i,x\) must shift every later element one place right, so it costs O\(n - i\); measured)
/E (list.insert\(i,x\) must shift every later element one place right, so it costs O\(n - i\); measured)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [59]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 114 0 R
/T (81 us in the middle and 176 us at the front of 365,000 items. For one reading in a 365-item year this is a few microseconds - negligible - so a list is still fine for the village station. A better design, though, avoids inserting at all: pre-fill the list with one slot per day \(None for a missing reading\) so a late reading is simply readings[day] = value, O\(1\). If readings arrive out of order frequently and in large numbers, a dictionary keyed by date is the better structure.)
/E (81 us in the middle and 176 us at the front of 365,000 items. For one reading in a 365-item year this is a few microseconds - negligible - so a list is still fine for the village station. A better design, though, avoids inserting at all: pre-fill the list with one slot per day \(None for a missing reading\) so a late reading is simply readings[day] = value, O\(1\). If readings arrive out of order frequently and in large numbers, a dictionary keyed by date is the better structure.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [60]
>>
endobj
