387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 116 0 R
/T (Alias \(b = a\): the same object under two names. Shallow copy \(copy.copy\(a\), a[:]\): a new outer container whose items are the same objects. Deep copy \(copy.deepcopy\(a\)\): new copies of every nested mutable object. The output shows the three behaviours: after orig.append\([5, 6]\) and orig[0][0] = 99, the alias sees both changes, the shallow copies see only the inner change \(shared inner list\), and the deep copy sees neither. A shallow copy is enough when the items are immutable \(numbers, strings, tuples\) or you will never modify the nested objects; a deep copy is needed for nested mutable data, such as a grid of lists, when copies must be independent.)
/E (Alias \(b = a\): the same object under two names. Shallow copy \(copy.copy\(a\), a[:]\): a new outer container whose items are the same objects. Deep copy \(copy.deepcopy\(a\)\): new copies of every nested mutable object. The output shows the three behaviours: after orig.append\([5, 6]\) and orig[0][0] = 99, the alias sees both changes, the shallow copies see only the inner change \(shared inner list\), and the deep copy sees neither. A shallow copy is enough when the items are immutable \(numbers, strings, tuples\) or you will never modify the nested objects; a deep copy is needed for nested mutable data, such as a grid of lists, when copies must be independent.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 116 0 R
/T (Why a function changes the caller's list. Python passes references to objects. Inside the function the parameter name is bound to the same list object as the caller's variable \(the ids in the output are equal\), so route.append\(\) mutates the shared object. Rebinding the parameter \(route = route + [...]\) creates a new list and the caller is unaffected - so the function must return it.)
/E (Why a function changes the caller's list. Python passes references to objects. Inside the function the parameter name is bound to the same list object as the caller's variable \(the ids in the output are equal\), so route.append\(\) mutates the shared object. Rebinding the parameter \(route = route + [...]\) creates a new list and the caller is unaffected - so the function must return it.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
