/T (Reading the results. The sizes differ by only 16 bytes - the list object header is slightly larger \(a list built by append would also hold spare capacity\). getsizeof is shallow: the million int objects themselves are not counted and are shared by both. Creating and indexing are within a few percent of each other, so speed is not a reason to choose a tuple at this scale. The list-inside-a-tuple test: t[0].append\(99\) succeeds because the tuple only promises that its slots never point to a different object; the list object itself is still mutable. t[0] += [5] is the surprising case: the list is extended in place first, then the assignment back to t[0] raises TypeError - so the error is raised and the change happened.)
