/T (Use a tuple for a fixed record of a few related values that should not change - a \(latitude, longitude\) pair, an \(r, g, b\) colour, a \(station, arrival, departure\) row - and whenever a value must be hashable \(dictionary key, set member\). Use a list for a collection that grows, shrinks or is edited. Immutability protects you from accidental modification through aliases and function arguments, and from corrupting a dictionary whose keys would otherwise change after insertion. It is a shallow guarantee: it does not make a mutable object inside the tuple immutable.)
/E (Use a tuple for a fixed record of a few related values that should not change - a \(latitude, longitude\) pair, an \(r, g, b\) colour, a \(station, arrival, departure\) row - and whenever a value must be hashable \(dictionary key, set member\). Use a list for a collection that grows, shrinks or is edited. Immutability protects you from accidental modification through aliases and function arguments, and from corrupting a dictionary whose keys would otherwise change after insertion. It is a shallow guarantee: it does not make a mutable object inside the tuple immutable.)
