>>
endobj
822 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 124 0 R
/T (appends expression for each item that satisfies condition. When a comprehension is harder to read than a loop: when it needs more than one condition plus a nested loop, when the expression is long or has side effects, when the same value is computed twice, or when it has to build two lists at once - a loop with comments is clearer then. Dictionary and set comprehensions \(from the demonstration\): {m: x for m, x in enumerate\(r\) if LOW <= x <= HIGH} maps minute to temperature; {round\(x\) for x in r if LOW <= x <= HIGH} gives the distinct rounded temperatures. List comprehension vs generator expression: square brackets build the whole list in memory at once; round brackets create a generator that produces one value at a time on demand - it can be used once, cannot be indexed, but uses constant memory.)
/E (appends expression for each item that satisfies condition. When a comprehension is harder to read than a loop: when it needs more than one condition plus a nested loop, when the expression is long or has side effects, when the same value is computed twice, or when it has to build two lists at once - a loop with comments is clearer then. Dictionary and set comprehensions \(from the demonstration\): {m: x for m, x in enumerate\(r\) if LOW <= x <= HIGH} maps minute to temperature; {round\(x\) for x in r if LOW <= x <= HIGH} gives the distinct rounded temperatures. List comprehension vs generator expression: square brackets build the whole list in memory at once; round brackets create a generator that produces one value at a time on demand - it can be used once, cannot be indexed, but uses constant memory.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
