/T (4. Conclusion - is a list comprehension always best?)
/E (4. Conclusion - is a list comprehension always best?)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [77]
>>
endobj
918 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 125 0 R
/T (No. At 1,000,000 readings the plain loop was the fastest \(0.302 s\) and the comprehension was 11% slower \(0.336 s\), with map/filter slowest \(0.366 s\). The comprehension version here makes three passes over the data \(the filter condition is evaluated for the Celsius list, again for the Fahrenheit list, and again for the alarms\) while the loop makes two, so the comprehension's usual speed advantage from avoiding append look-ups is cancelled by the extra work. The slowest is 21% slower than the fastest, and all give identical results, so the decision should rest on readability and memory. The generator expression, where the result is consumed once \(a sum, a count\), is the clear winner on memory - 8,448,720 bytes against 416 bytes at the same speed. A comprehension is best for building one list from one pass with a simple condition; a loop is better when several results are produced together; a generator is better when no list is needed.)
/E (No. At 1,000,000 readings the plain loop was the fastest \(0.302 s\) and the comprehension was 11% slower \(0.336 s\), with map/filter slowest \(0.366 s\). The comprehension version here makes three passes over the data \(the filter condition is evaluated for the Celsius list, again for the Fahrenheit list, and again for the alarms\) while the loop makes two, so the comprehension's usual speed advantage from avoiding append look-ups is cancelled by the extra work. The slowest is 21% slower than the fastest, and all give identical results, so the decision should rest on readability and memory. The generator expression, where the result is consumed once \(a sum, a count\), is the clear winner on memory - 8,448,720 bytes against 416 bytes at the same speed. A comprehension is best for building one list from one pass with a simple condition; a loop is better when several results are produced together; a generator is better when no list is needed.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [78]
>>
endobj
