922 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 126 0 R
/T (sum\(c for c in readings if -50 <= c <= 50\) replaces sum\([c for c in readings if ...]\). Time is O\(n\) in both - one pass - and the measured times are practically equal \(0.078 s and 0.074 s\). Memory differs: the list comprehension materialises up to n values, O\(n\) \(8.4 MB for about 970,000 valid readings\), while the generator holds only its current state, O\(1\) \(416 bytes\). The cost of the generator is that it can be consumed only once; to use the cleaned readings twice \(for example to print and to convert to Fahrenheit\) a list is needed.)
/E (sum\(c for c in readings if -50 <= c <= 50\) replaces sum\([c for c in readings if ...]\). Time is O\(n\) in both - one pass - and the measured times are practically equal \(0.078 s and 0.074 s\). Memory differs: the list comprehension materialises up to n values, O\(n\) \(8.4 MB for about 970,000 valid readings\), while the generator holds only its current state, O\(1\) \(416 bytes\). The cost of the generator is that it can be consumed only once; to use the cleaned readings twice \(for example to print and to convert to Fahrenheit\) a list is needed.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
