1082 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 129 0 R
/T (One flat list of tuples \(train, station, arrival, departure, days\) scanned for every query: [r for r in flat if r[1] == station and day in r[4]]. Time per query is O\(total stops\) - the same order as the nested scan, and measured slightly faster \(2.78 ms against 3.52 ms at 10,000 trains\) because there is no nesting to walk - but 11x slower than the index. Space is one 5-field tuple per stop \(the days set can be shared\), O\(total stops\), with the train name and days repeated or referenced per row; the nested design stores each train's data once and adds the index's O\(total stops\) of extra entries. The flat list is the simplest design and fine for a few hundred trains; the nested dictionaries with an index are the better design once queries dominate.)
/E (One flat list of tuples \(train, station, arrival, departure, days\) scanned for every query: [r for r in flat if r[1] == station and day in r[4]]. Time per query is O\(total stops\) - the same order as the nested scan, and measured slightly faster \(2.78 ms against 3.52 ms at 10,000 trains\) because there is no nesting to walk - but 11x slower than the index. Space is one 5-field tuple per stop \(the days set can be shared\), O\(total stops\), with the train name and days repeated or referenced per row; the nested design stores each train's data once and adds the index's O\(total stops\) of extra entries. The flat list is the simplest design and fine for a few hundred trains; the nested dictionaries with an index are the better design once queries dominate.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1083 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 129 0 R
/T (Reading the results. Without the index, query time grows in proportion to the number of stops: it is about 141x higher for 100 times more trains \(stops grew 102x\). With the index it also grows, because each station is served by more trains in a bigger timetable \(the experiment keeps the number of stations fixed at 200\), so k grows with T; the speed-up shrinks from 31x to 14x as a result. The speed-up is bounded by roughly the number of stations; a national timetable has thousands of stations, so the gain there would be far larger. The two special cases pass: an unknown station returns an empty list in both methods, and a train that does not run on the requested day is filtered out \(12295 on Sunday\). Extra memory used by the index at 10,000 trains: 8.35 MB.)
/E (Reading the results. Without the index, query time grows in proportion to the number of stops: it is about 141x higher for 100 times more trains \(stops grew 102x\). With the index it also grows, because each station is served by more trains in a bigger timetable \(the experiment keeps the number of stations fixed at 200\), so k grows with T; the speed-up shrinks from 31x to 14x as a result. The speed-up is bounded by roughly the number of stations; a national timetable has thousands of stations, so the gain there would be far larger. The two special cases pass: an unknown station returns an empty list in both methods, and a train that does not run on the requested day is filtered out \(12295 on Sunday\). Extra memory used by the index at 10,000 trains: 8.35 MB.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [6]
>>
