1251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 132 0 R
/T (Hand verification. Of the 7 test contacts, #1, #2 and #3 are one person: #1 and #2 share 9840012345 \(written "+91 98400 12345" and "9840012345"\), and #2 and #3 share 9444555555 \("094445 55555" and "\(+91\) 94445-55555"\); #4 and #5 share an e-mail \(case and a trailing space differ\); #6 has the same name as #2 but a different number, and stays separate; #7 has an unusable number and nothing else, so it is kept alone as an empty record. Expected: 4 records. The program returns 4, identical to the pairwise method. \(Writing this hand case first caught a typo in my own test data - a number with the wrong digit count - which the program had correctly refused to merge.\) The time per contact rises from 6 to 12 microseconds between 1,000 and 100,000 contacts; the algorithm is linear, and the extra is memory effects \(larger dictionaries do not fit in the processor cache\) and garbage collection.)
/E (Hand verification. Of the 7 test contacts, #1, #2 and #3 are one person: #1 and #2 share 9840012345 \(written "+91 98400 12345" and "9840012345"\), and #2 and #3 share 9444555555 \("094445 55555" and "\(+91\) 94445-55555"\); #4 and #5 share an e-mail \(case and a trailing space differ\); #6 has the same name as #2 but a different number, and stays separate; #7 has an unusable number and nothing else, so it is kept alone as an empty record. Expected: 4 records. The program returns 4, identical to the pairwise method. \(Writing this hand case first caught a typo in my own test data - a number with the wrong digit count - which the program had correctly refused to merge.\) The time per contact rises from 6 to 12 microseconds between 1,000 and 100,000 contacts; the algorithm is linear, and the extra is memory effects \(larger dictionaries do not fit in the processor cache\) and garbage collection.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
