/Title (The alternative is the nested loop for i: for j > i: if keys[i] & keys[j]: union\(i, j\) \(merge_pairwise in the source\). Time is O\(n^2\); space is O\(n\) for the key sets and the union-find array - the same order as the dictionary version, which has O\(n\) space too \(the owner dictionary adds one entry per distinct key\). The pairwise method was run up to 4,000 contacts, where it was 25x slower than the dictionary and the ratio was still growing, as it must for O\(n^2\) against O\(n\) \(doubling n quadruples the pairwise time - 0.17 s to 0.72 s from 2,000 to 4,000\). For 100,000 contacts the pairwise method was not run. From its measured rate at 4,000 contacts \(11.5 million pairs per second\), the 4,999,950,000 pairs would take an estimated 436 s \(about 7 minutes\) - an extrapolation, not a measurement, and likely optimistic because larger inputs are slower per operation - against the measured 0.93 s for the dictionary, roughly 469x less.)
/Dest [32 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1266 0 R
/Prev 1578 0 R
>>
