1575 0 obj
<<
/Title (Why a dictionary beats comparing every pair. Finding duplicates by comparison needs n\(n-1\)/2 pairs; at 100,000 contacts that is about five billion. With a dictionary each contact does a few O\(1\) look-ups of its keys, so the total work is proportional to n. The measurements \(next section\) agree: the dictionary took 0.9 s for 100,000 contacts.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1447 0 R
/Next 1576 0 R
>>
endobj
1576 0 obj
<<
/Title (Two different people with the same name. Names are never a matching key. Two "Ravi Kumar" contacts with different numbers get different keys, are never joined, and so stay separate records \(R3; contact #6 in the test\). The names are kept as a set per record only for display. Remaining limitations. \(a\) The design can only link contacts through a shared number or e-mail: the same person saved once under an old number and once under a new one, with nothing in common, appears as two people \(this is why 100,000 generated contacts give more records than people\). \(b\) A number shared by several people - a family landline or an office switchboard - will wrongly merge them, because the rule is transitive. \(c\) Only Indian +91 / 0-prefixed numbers are normalised. \(d\) Contacts with only a name remain unmerged.)
/Dest [31 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1447 0 R
/Prev 1575 0 R
>>
endobj
