/Title (One flat list of tuples \(train, station, arrival, departure, days\) scanned for every query: [r for r in flat if r[1] == station and day in r[4]]. Time per query is O\(total stops\) - the same order as the nested scan, and measured slightly faster \(2.78 ms against 3.52 ms at 10,000 trains\) because there is no nesting to walk - but 11x slower than the index. Space is one 5-field tuple per stop \(the days set can be shared\), O\(total stops\), with the train name and days repeated or referenced per row; the nested design stores each train's data once and adds the index's O\(total stops\) of extra entries. The flat list is the simplest design and fine for a few hundred trains; the nested dictionaries with an index are the better design once queries dominate.)
