<<
/Title (How a dictionary finds a key. Python computes hash\(key\), uses part of it as an index into an internal table, and looks at that slot. If the slot holds an equal key the value is returned; if it holds a different key \(a collision\) Python probes a deterministic sequence of other slots until it finds the key or an empty slot. The table is resized to keep it at most about two-thirds full, so the expected number of probes is a small constant. The cost therefore depends on the quality of the hash and the load factor, not on how many keys are stored - that is why the average lookup is O\(1\) whether the dictionary holds ten words or a million.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1964 0 R
/Next 1984 0 R
>>
endobj
1984 0 obj
<<
/Title (Dictionary vs list.count\(\). The dictionary visits every word once \(n words, O\(1\) each\) = O\(n\). words.count\(w\) scans the whole list for each of the d distinct words = O\(n x d\). Key order. From Python 3.7 a dict keeps keys in insertion order \(guaranteed by the language\); re-assigning an existing key does not move it. This is order of insertion, not sorted order. Views. keys\(\), values\(\) and items\(\) return live view objects, not copies - the output shows keys\(\) reflecting a key added after the view was created. They cost O\(1\) to create and O\(n\) to iterate; items\(\) yields \(key, value\) tuples.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1964 0 R
/Prev 1983 0 R
>>
endobj
