/Title (General form: [expressionfor item in iterable if condition], equivalent to a loop that)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2029 0 R
/Next 2056 0 R
>>
endobj
2056 0 obj
<<
/Title (appends expression for each item that satisfies condition. When a comprehension is harder to read than a loop: when it needs more than one condition plus a nested loop, when the expression is long or has side effects, when the same value is computed twice, or when it has to build two lists at once - a loop with comments is clearer then. Dictionary and set comprehensions \(from the demonstration\): {m: x for m, x in enumerate\(r\) if LOW <= x <= HIGH} maps minute to temperature; {round\(x\) for x in r if LOW <= x <= HIGH} gives the distinct rounded temperatures. List comprehension vs generator expression: square brackets build the whole list in memory at once; round brackets create a generator that produces one value at a time on demand - it can be used once, cannot be indexed, but uses constant memory.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2029 0 R
/Prev 2055 0 R
>>
endobj
